# Copy this file to tools/github/.env and fill in real values:
#   cp .env.example .env
# tools/github/.env is gitignored — never commit it. See
# docs/04-contracts/security-and-secrets.md.

# --- Auth token ------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no fixed variable name like GITHUB_TOKEN. Pick any name you like,
# but it must end in `__<TYPE>` (two underscores + a type suffix) — that's
# how the control panel (`bun run dev:panel`) recognizes a line here as a
# candidate credential for this server. `AUTH` is the only type the github
# server currently reads (tools/github/src/index.ts), so a token you want it
# to use must be suffixed `__AUTH`, e.g. GITHUB_TOKEN__AUTH below.
#
# You can list several tokens at once — one per account, one per scope,
# whatever you need:
#   GITHUB_TOKEN_WORK__AUTH=
#   GITHUB_TOKEN_PERSONAL__AUTH=
#
# Adding a line here only makes the value available; it does nothing by
# itself. In the control panel: register the key as github's `auth` token,
# then mark exactly one of them active. The active row's *name* is what gets
# looked up in process.env at request time, so switching which one is active
# takes effect on the server's next tool call — no restart needed. With none
# active, calls run unauthenticated: public repos only, 60 requests/hour.
#
# Scope it narrow — see "Scoping a GitHub token" in
# docs/04-contracts/security-and-secrets.md. A classic token needs `repo`;
# fine-grained `Issues: read-only` is enough for the read-only tools.
GITHUB_TOKEN__AUTH=

# --- Optional ----------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub login the "@me" sentinel resolves to in search queries.
GITHUB_DEFAULT_USERNAME=

# --- Configured elsewhere, not here -------------------------------------------
# Default owner/repository: add and activate a profile in the control panel
# (the github_profiles table) instead of an env var here.
# Write/destructive tool access: the permission table in the control panel —
# one allow/deny/ask row per tool. See ADR-0008 and ADR-0009. There is no
# env-var write flag anymore.
